=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router2
diagnosis_text:
**Affected component:** The FRR BGP daemon (`bgpd`) on router2 is not running. The watchfrr supervisor is also absent, so there is no automatic restart mechanism in place.

**Mechanism:** router1 (AS 1) is correctly configured with `bgpd` running and advertises the 195.11.14.0/24 prefix. It attempts to open a TCP session on port 179 to router2, but because router2's `bgpd` process is dead, no one listens — the session stays in the BGP **Active** state indefinitely. Symmetrically, router2 never advertises 200.1.1.0/24 to router1. With no BGP-derived routes on either router, both return ICMP "Destination Net Unreachable" to their respective hosts when they try to forward inter-AS traffic. The direct-IP pings from pc1 → 200.1.1.2 and pc2 → 195.11.14.2 both confirm 100% loss with the error originating from the local gateway.

**Localization:** router2, FRR control-plane process (`bgpd`). The inter-AS WAN link (router1:eth0 ↔ router2:eth0) is physically intact — both routers are responsive at the IP level.

**Root cause:** `bgpd` (and its watchfrr supervisor) crashed or was never started on router2, causing the BGP session to never establish and all inter-AS routes to be absent from both routing tables.

**Fix:** Restart FRR on router2 (`systemctl restart frr` or `service frr restart`). This brings up `bgpd` and `watchfrr`, the BGP session with router1 will establish, routes will be exchanged, and pc1 ↔ pc2 connectivity will be restored. No re-cabling is needed.
=== END ===